{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Behold, you lie down with your fathers.</b> They said in the Midrash: When the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, ״הן קרבו ימיך למות״ — 'Behold, your days draw near for death' — Moses said to Him: Master of the Universe! With 'הן' I praised You, as it is said (Deuteronomy 10:14): ״הן לה' אלהיך השמים ושמי השמים״ — 'Behold, to HaShem your Elohim belong the heavens and the highest heavens' — yet with that very expression You impose death upon me! He said to him: I will make for you contentment of spirit. Immediately the utterance came to him from the blessed Name and said: ״הנך שוכב עם אבותיך״ — 'Behold, you lie down with your fathers.' The expression 'הנך' is nothing but contentment of spirit." "<b>And in the tractate</b> of Sanhedrin they interpreted: ״הנך שוכב וקם״ — 'you lie down and arise' — from here is a proof for the resurrection of the dead from the Torah. And in a similar manner they interpreted the verse (Exodus 15:1): ״אז ישיר משה״ — 'Then Moses will sing' — 'sang' (שר) is not written, but rather 'will sing' (ישיר); from here is a hint to the resurrection of the dead from the Torah. And the explanation of what they said, 'I will make for you contentment of spirit' — in my opinion this is the contentment of spirit of the World to Come, which He would promise him therein; and in the manner of what they said: one hour of contentment of spirit in the World to Come is finer than all the life of this world. For Moses praised Him with 'הן' and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״הן קרבו״ — 'behold, they draw near' — and this drawing near is toward eternal life, for death is the cause of life and the gateway to the World to Come. If so, this is not an accusation and a punishment but rather a reward, for the upright King would not cut off the head of his minister with the very sword he brought him as a gift; rather, he would recompense him and give him gifts. And so the Holy One, blessed be He, recompensed Moses here in accordance with His mercies and the abundance of His lovingkindnesses — thus He said to him: You praised Me with 'הן' and I say to you ״הן קרבו ימיך״ and ״הנך״, which is contentment of spirit for the World to Come, that your soul will be stored away beneath the Throne of Glory. And this is the additional ״כ״ in the word 'הנך' (ה+נ=ך), for it is the Throne of Glory; and as I hinted with the additional ״כ״ of (Leviticus 27:2): ״בערכך נפשות לה'״ — 'when you assess souls to HaShem' — for the souls that are 'to HaShem' are assessed by the measure of the Throne of Glory. You find thereby that even though He mentioned death to him in the word 'הן', He included therein the true life toward which he was drawing near, and He foretold him two kinds of life: the first, the life of the World of Souls, which draws near to a man immediately upon his death; and the second, the life of the World of Resurrection, from which he will be transported to the World to Come, which is the ultimate reward on the great Sabbath day, and there they will merit and delight in body and in soul. And this is what He said: ״הנך״ — 'behold you' — with regard to the soul in the World of Souls, that it will be stored away there in the Throne of Glory; and He said ״שוכב וקם״ — 'lie down and arise' — with regard to the body, for the time of the resurrection of the dead. And this is the explanation of the contentment of spirit that He told him, and this is clear." "<b>Strange gods of the land.</b> The verse explains that the gods are strangers in the land of HaShem, because it is His inheritance and He did not appoint over it any angel or seraph or star or constellation or any force among the supernal forces. And this is what they said: ״אין מזל לישראל״ — 'Israel has no constellation.' And the intent of this is not that the constellations do not cause things for Israel, for they explicitly said ״מזלו גורם״ — 'his constellation causes.' Rather, the matter is that Israel is not given over to the governance of the constellations, because the stars and constellations and the entire world were created for the sake of the righteous — if so, how could the lower be appointed over the higher than it, and the effect over the cause? Rather, they are appointed over idolaters, for every star and constellation is appointed over its own nation. <b>And likewise</b> they said in the books of the distinguished wisdom, which is the wisdom of the stars: the constellation Scorpio over the lands of Ishmael; Sagittarius over Persia; Capricorn over the Philistines; Virgo or Libra over Edom. And this is what the verse said (Deuteronomy 4:19–20): ״אשר חלק ה' אלהיך אותם לכל העמים תחת כל השמים, ואתכם לקח ה'״ — 'which HaShem your Elohim has allotted to all the peoples under all the heavens; but you has HaShem taken' — explaining that the Holy One, blessed be He, gave them as their allotment, not as Israel's allotment. And therefore we do not find anywhere in the entire Torah that the verse holds the nations accountable for the matter of idolatry — only Israel, who are singled out for the allotment of the Exalted One. And likewise we do not find punishment for the nations for serving idolatry unless they served it in the Holy Land; and the proof is from the Cutheans, who were not punished for serving idols in their own land until they came to the Land and served it, and then they were punished, as it is said (II Kings 17:26): ״וישלח בם את האריות והנם ממיתים אותן כי לא ידעו משפט אלהי הארץ״ — 'And He sent lions among them and they were killing them, for they did not know the law of the god of the land.' And therefore it says here 'gods, strangers of the land,' for the gods and all the forces of idolatry are strangers in the Land, and everyone who serves idolatry is like one who seeks to drive the King from His palace. And this is the superiority of the Land of Israel, and it is the secret of the verse said concerning the rain (Job 5:10): ״הנותן מטר על פני ארץ ושולח מים על פני חוצות״ — 'Who gives rain upon the face of the earth and sends waters upon the face of the streets.' The verse teaches you that the essence of providence is in the Land, and from there it extends to the other places of the world; and therefore it says ״בארץ״ — 'in the land' — ״הנותן״ — 'Who gives' — that is, He Himself; but in the other places, which He called 'streets' (חוצות), He said ״ושולח״ — 'and sends' — by means of an emissary. And the fact that He called them 'streets' (חוצות) alludes to the inwardness of the Land's superiority — that is, that they are outside the King's palace. And David, peace be upon him, called them 'tents of wickedness'; as he said (Psalms 84:11): ״בחרתי הסתופף בבית אלהי מדור באהלי רשע״ — 'I have chosen to stand at the threshold of the house of my Elohim rather than dwell in the tents of wickedness' — that is, I have chosen the sitting of the threshold over dwelling outside the Land, which are 'tents of wickedness'; and corresponding to Jerusalem, which is the essence of justice, he called the other lands 'tents of wickedness.' And this chapter concerns the First Temple, for there Israel served idolatry." "<b>Which he comes there in its midst.</b> That is, in the midst of the Land, for thus we find 'ארץ' in the masculine, as (Genesis 13:6): ״ולא נשא אותם הארץ״ — 'and the land could not bear them.' Or it may allude by 'in its midst' to the midst of the Temple, as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'Until when? Until they brought idolatry into the Temple, as it is said (Ezekiel 8:5): ״סמל הקנאה הזה בבאה״ — 'this image of jealousy at the entrance.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:07.626308839Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}